WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.815
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level four.

2
00:00:06.115 --> 00:00:09.489
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.789 --> 00:00:15.809
Unit four: Climate Diplomacy. Today you will set future targets with the future perfect.

4
00:00:16.109 --> 00:00:18.680
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.633 --> 00:00:22.878
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.717 --> 00:00:25.054
Climate accord.

7
00:00:26.050 --> 00:00:30.116
Climate accord means an international agreement to fight climate change.

8
00:00:30.717 --> 00:00:33.006
The climate accord set a limit on warming.

9
00:00:33.617 --> 00:00:36.873
Your turn. Say: Climate accord.

10
00:00:39.733 --> 00:00:41.152
Climate accord.

11
00:00:41.917 --> 00:00:43.371
Carbon emissions.

12
00:00:44.383 --> 00:00:49.239
Carbon emissions means the carbon gases released into the air by human activity.

13
00:00:49.850 --> 00:00:52.813
Carbon emissions fell after the factory reforms.

14
00:00:53.417 --> 00:00:56.301
Your turn. Say: Carbon emissions.

15
00:00:59.167 --> 00:01:00.504
Carbon emissions.

16
00:01:01.250 --> 00:01:02.901
Sustainable development.

17
00:01:03.917 --> 00:01:08.459
Sustainable development means growth that meets today's needs without harming the future.

18
00:01:09.050 --> 00:01:12.141
Sustainable development protects forests and jobs.

19
00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:15.936
Your turn. Say: Sustainable development.

20
00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:20.544
Sustainable development.

21
00:01:21.283 --> 00:01:22.331
Global warming.

22
00:01:23.317 --> 00:01:27.034
Global warming means the gradual rise in Earth's average temperature.

23
00:01:27.650 --> 00:01:29.975
Global warming is melting glaciers.

24
00:01:30.583 --> 00:01:33.305
Your turn. Say: Global warming.

25
00:01:36.167 --> 00:01:37.655
Global warming.

26
00:01:38.417 --> 00:01:39.894
Renewable transition.

27
00:01:40.883 --> 00:01:45.623
Renewable transition means the move from fossil fuels to clean energy sources.

28
00:01:46.217 --> 00:01:49.180
The renewable transition created new industries.

29
00:01:49.783 --> 00:01:53.005
Your turn. Say: Renewable transition.

30
00:01:55.867 --> 00:01:57.460
Renewable transition.

31
00:01:58.217 --> 00:02:00.158
Environmental treaty.

32
00:02:01.150 --> 00:02:05.448
Environmental treaty means a legal agreement between countries to protect nature.

33
00:02:06.050 --> 00:02:09.478
The environmental treaty bans harmful chemicals.

34
00:02:10.083 --> 00:02:13.664
Your turn. Say: Environmental treaty.

35
00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:18.918
Environmental treaty.

36
00:02:19.683 --> 00:02:21.056
Climate justice.

37
00:02:22.050 --> 00:02:26.766
Climate justice means the idea that those who caused least harm should be protected most.

38
00:02:27.350 --> 00:02:29.976
Climate justice supports the poorest nations.

39
00:02:30.583 --> 00:02:33.770
Your turn. Say: Climate justice.

40
00:02:36.633 --> 00:02:38.134
Climate justice.

41
00:02:38.883 --> 00:02:40.616
Adaptation strategy.

42
00:02:41.617 --> 00:02:46.124
Adaptation strategy means a plan for coping with the effects of climate change.

43
00:02:46.717 --> 00:02:49.900
An adaptation strategy includes new sea walls.

44
00:02:50.517 --> 00:02:54.121
Your turn. Say: Adaptation strategy.

45
00:02:56.967 --> 00:02:58.664
Adaptation strategy.

46
00:02:59.467 --> 00:03:03.187
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:10.719
Visal, a minister says 'By 2050, we will have cut carbon emissions by half.' What tense is 'will have cut'?

48
00:03:11.817 --> 00:03:22.991
The future perfect: will have + past participle. It describes an action completed before a future time, shown by 'by 2050' or 'by the end of the decade'.

49
00:03:24.083 --> 00:03:26.036
Is there a continuous form?

50
00:03:27.150 --> 00:03:39.009
Yes, will have been + -ing stresses duration: 'By 2030, countries will have been negotiating for forty years.' We use it for long processes.

51
00:03:40.117 --> 00:03:41.884
How is it different from will cut?

52
00:03:42.983 --> 00:03:51.290
'Will cut' is a plan or prediction without a deadline. 'Will have cut' promises the result by a deadline, so it sounds like a firm target.

53
00:03:52.433 --> 00:03:53.829
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:54.683 --> 00:03:58.982
Grammar focus: Future perfect: will have plus past participle.

55
00:03:59.282 --> 00:04:00.480
Look at the pattern.

56
00:04:01.183 --> 00:04:04.971
Nations will have cut carbon emissions by half by 2050.

57
00:04:05.883 --> 00:04:10.216
The renewable transition will have replaced most coal plants by the end of the decade.

58
00:04:11.117 --> 00:04:15.171
Diplomats will have been negotiating for forty years by 2030.

59
00:04:16.133 --> 00:04:25.519
Use will have + past participle for something completed before a future deadline (by 2050). Use will have been + -ing to show how long something has continued.

60
00:04:26.283 --> 00:04:30.271
Be careful! Don't say: By 2050 we will have reduce it.

61
00:04:32.233 --> 00:04:35.138
Say: By 2050 we will have reduced it.

62
00:04:35.438 --> 00:04:37.810
Use the past participle after will have.

63
00:04:38.600 --> 00:04:41.740
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:42.533 --> 00:04:43.987
Targets and Deadlines.

65
00:04:44.287 --> 00:04:48.748
Every climate accord rests on promises about what will happen by a certain date.

66
00:04:49.733 --> 00:04:56.899
By 2030, many countries will have reduced carbon emissions, and some will have completed their renewable transition.

67
00:04:57.900 --> 00:05:03.917
By then, negotiators will have been meeting for decades to turn an environmental treaty into real change.

68
00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:11.137
Climate justice means that rich countries, which have emitted most, should have funded adaptation strategies long before.

69
00:05:12.133 --> 00:05:18.696
Sustainable development will only succeed if promises become results before global warming passes dangerous limits.

70
00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:23.802
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:24.650 --> 00:05:27.625
By 2040, scientists blank the project.

72
00:05:27.925 --> 00:05:32.356
A, will complete. B, will have completed. C, are completing.

73
00:05:35.900 --> 00:05:38.654
The answer is B: will have completed.

74
00:05:39.817 --> 00:05:42.060
Which form stresses a long duration?

75
00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:47.627
A, will have been negotiating. B, will negotiate. C, will have negotiated.

76
00:05:51.167 --> 00:05:54.200
The answer is A: will have been negotiating.

77
00:05:55.350 --> 00:05:59.184
A plan to cope with the effects of climate change is an blank strategy.

78
00:05:59.484 --> 00:06:03.172
A, adaptation. B, emission. C, election.

79
00:06:06.733 --> 00:06:08.710
The answer is A: adaptation.

80
00:06:09.850 --> 00:06:11.652
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:06:11.952 --> 00:06:21.387
A, By 2030 we will have reduce emissions.. B, By 2030 we will have reduced emissions.. C, By 2030 we have will reduced emissions..

82
00:06:24.933 --> 00:06:29.417
The answer is B: By 2030 we will have reduced emissions..

83
00:06:30.583 --> 00:06:33.570
According to the reading, what must promises become?

84
00:06:33.870 --> 00:06:37.604
A, results. B, slogans. C, debates.

85
00:06:41.167 --> 00:06:43.282
The answer is A: results.

86
00:06:44.467 --> 00:06:48.036
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:48.883 --> 00:06:53.147
Why is international cooperation especially important for climate issues?

88
00:07:01.950 --> 00:07:03.334
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:07:03.634 --> 00:07:30.264
Climate change is a shared problem because carbon emissions mix in the atmosphere and affect every country, whoever produced them. One nation acting alone cannot stop global warming. Through a climate accord, countries can agree fair targets, share clean technology and fund adaptation strategies for those most at risk, which supports climate justice.

90
00:07:30.564 --> 00:07:32.320
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:33.217 --> 00:07:38.957
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and future perfect: will have plus past participle.

92
00:07:39.257 --> 00:07:44.417
Today's value is sustainability. Choose ways of living that protect the planet for the future.

93
00:07:44.717 --> 00:07:47.486
See you in Unit five. Goodbye!

94
00:07:47.786 --> 00:07:50.740
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

